-- Q5520: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 7872, below-market loans between family members are treated as if the lender made a gift equal to the forgone interest. For a demand loan of $800,000 at 0% when the AFR is 4.5%, the imputed interest is $800,000 x 4.5% = $36,000 per year, treated as a taxable gift.
Wrong (A): Section 7872 specifically subjects family loans to gift tax by recharacterizing the forgone interest as a deemed transfer from lender to borrower.
Wrong (C): Only the forgone interest is treated as a gift, not the full principal. The loan itself is not reclassified as a gift unless it is forgiven.
Wrong (D): The $19,000 annual exclusion is a separate concept. The imputed gift is $36,000 before applying any exclusion, not limited to it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5520;

-- Q5522: Estate and Gift Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6166, if a closely held business interest exceeds 35% of the adjusted gross estate, the executor may elect to pay estate tax attributable to that interest in installments over up to 14 years, with an initial deferral period of up to 5 years. This provides liquidity relief for estates holding illiquid business assets.
Wrong (B): Section 6166 does not exempt the business interest from estate tax. It only allows the tax payment to be spread over time through installments.
Wrong (C): There is no special reduced estate tax rate for business assets. The normal estate tax rate schedule applies regardless of asset type.
Wrong (D): The business interest remains in the gross estate and is fully subject to estate tax. Section 6166 addresses payment timing, not inclusion.$EXPL$
