-- Q5524: Estate and Gift Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 2511 and Treas. Reg. Section 25.2511-2, a gift is incomplete when the donor retains dominion and control over the property, such as the power to revoke the trust or redirect assets to different beneficiaries. An incomplete gift is not subject to gift tax at the time of transfer.
Wrong (A): A Crummey power is held by the beneficiary, not the donor. It actually makes the transfer a completed gift of a present interest rather than an incomplete one.
Wrong (C): Exceeding the annual exclusion does not make a gift incomplete. It simply means the excess is a taxable gift subject to the unified credit.
Wrong (D): The donee''s age is irrelevant to gift completeness. Gifts to minors can be complete if the donor relinquishes dominion and control.$EXPL$
