  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 2042(2), life insurance proceeds are included in the insured''s gross estate when the decedent possessed any incidents of ownership at death, such as the power to change beneficiaries, borrow against the policy, or surrender it. Dawson held all three powers, so the full $2,000,000 is includable.
Wrong (A): Section 2042 looks at ownership incidents, not the named beneficiary. Proceeds payable to a third party are still includable if the insured retained control over the policy.
Wrong (B): Life insurance is not exempt from estate tax. Proceeds are includable under Section 2042 when the decedent held incidents of ownership or the proceeds are payable to the estate.
Wrong (D): The marital property 50% rule does not apply here. Section 2042 includes the full proceeds based on ownership incidents, not a split-ownership theory.$EXPL$
