-- Q5572: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1015-4, the donee''s basis in a part-gift, part-sale transaction is the greater of the amount paid or the donor''s adjusted basis. Reeves paid $100,000, which exceeds Calderon''s $60,000 adjusted basis, so Reeves''s basis is $100,000.
Wrong (A): The straight carryover basis rule under Section 1015 is modified when the donee provides consideration. The greater-of rule applies instead, and $100,000 exceeds the $60,000 carryover amount.
Wrong (C): Fair market value is not used as the donee''s basis in a part-gift, part-sale. FMV sets the ceiling for gain recognition but does not establish the basis.
Wrong (D): There is no adjustment that blends cost and gift element to reach $120,000. The regulation provides a clear greater-of test, not a combined calculation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5572;

-- Q7719: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 4958, excess benefit transactions trigger a 25% initial excise tax on the disqualified person, calculated on the $350,000 excess ($87,500). If the excess benefit is not corrected within the taxable period, an additional 200% tax ($700,000) is imposed on the disqualified person.
Wrong (A): The initial tax rate is 25%, not 10%. The 10% rate applies to organization managers who knowingly participate in the excess benefit transaction, not to the disqualified person.
Wrong (B): Intermediate sanctions under Section 4958 do not automatically revoke exempt status. The excise tax is imposed on the individual, and revocation is a separate IRS determination.
Wrong (D): The initial 25% tax is imposed on the disqualified person (Dr. Harris), not on the organization. The organization faces a separate 10% manager tax only if managers knowingly approved the transaction.$EXPL$
