-- Q7729: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 4945(g), grants by private foundations to individuals for travel, study, or similar purposes are taxable expenditures unless the foundation obtains advance IRS approval of its grant-making procedures. Without this approval, the $15,000 grant triggers a 20% excise tax on the foundation.
Wrong (A): Educational purpose alone does not exempt the grant. The procedural safeguard of advance IRS approval is required regardless of the grant''s charitable intent.
Wrong (C): A written acknowledgment from the student does not substitute for advance IRS approval. Section 4945 requires pre-approved objective selection criteria and reporting procedures.
Wrong (D): Self-dealing under Section 4941 involves transactions between the foundation and its disqualified persons. A grant to an unrelated student is not a self-dealing transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7729;

-- Q7730: Tax-Exempt Organizations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), an organization must pass both the organizational test and the operational test. New Dawn''s articles properly limit purposes, satisfying the organizational test. However, devoting 65% of activities to commercial consulting fails the operational test because using revenue for exempt purposes does not make the revenue-generating activity itself an exempt function.
Wrong (A): The operational test examines how the organization actually operates, not merely how it uses its income. Funding scholarships with consulting proceeds does not cure the operational failure.
Wrong (B): The organizational test looks at the governing documents, not the activities conducted. The articles correctly limit purposes to education and charity, so this test is satisfied.
Wrong (C): A 501(c)(3) may conduct some unrelated business activity. The issue is that 65% commercial activity means the organization is not operated primarily for exempt purposes, failing only the operational test.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7730;

-- Q7731: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6652(c)(1)(A), organizations with gross receipts under $1,186,000 face a penalty of $20 per day for each day the return is late, up to the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of gross receipts. For 90 days late, the penalty is $20 x 90 = $1,800.
Wrong (B): The $100-per-day rate applies to larger organizations with gross receipts of $1,186,000 or more. Community Bridge''s $800,000 in gross receipts falls below that threshold.
Wrong (C): The maximum penalty does not apply immediately. It is a cap on the total accumulated daily penalties, not a flat amount imposed upon failure to file.
Wrong (D): There is no $50-per-day penalty rate in Section 6652(c)(1)(A). The applicable rates are $20 per day for smaller organizations and $100 per day for larger ones.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7731;

-- Q7732: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), private inurement applies only to insiders with a personal and private interest in the organization. The brother-in-law has no formal role, so private inurement does not apply. However, the broader private benefit doctrine prohibits more than incidental benefit to any person, insider or not, and the $20,000 overpayment violates this standard.
Wrong (A): Private inurement requires a direct insider relationship with the organization. Indirect family connections without organizational authority do not create insider status.
Wrong (B): Although the brother-in-law is not an insider, the above-market contract confers impermissible private benefit. The private benefit doctrine reaches non-insiders.
Wrong (D): Both doctrines cannot apply simultaneously to the same person. Since the brother-in-law is not an insider, only private benefit applies, not private inurement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7732;

-- Q7735: Tax-Exempt Organizations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 509(a)(2), an organization must satisfy two prongs: more than one-third of support from contributions and related gross receipts, and no more than one-third from investment income and UBI. Community Workshop meets prong one ($250,000 / $550,000 = 45.5%) but fails prong two ($300,000 / $550,000 = 54.5% exceeds one-third).
Wrong (B): Meeting the first prong alone is insufficient. Both prongs must be satisfied simultaneously for Section 509(a)(2) classification.
Wrong (C): Program-related workshop fees do count as gross receipts from related activities. The failure is on the investment income prong, not the contribution prong.
Wrong (D): Investment income is not excluded from the support calculation. It is specifically included and capped at one-third of total support under the second prong.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7735;

-- Q7739: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 513(a)(2), activities conducted for the convenience of students, patients, or employees are excluded from UBIT. The university laundry serving dormitories qualifies for this exclusion, but the $40,000 from off-campus customers does not serve the university''s constituency and must be analyzed under the standard UBIT test.
Wrong (B): The convenience exclusion is based on who the activity serves, not where it is located. On-campus location alone does not exclude revenue from outside customers.
Wrong (C): Laundry services for dormitory students are related to the university''s educational mission under the convenience exclusion. Only the off-campus portion may be unrelated.
Wrong (D): There is no 25% de minimis threshold in the UBIT rules. Revenue from off-campus customers is analyzed separately regardless of its proportion to total revenue.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7739;

-- Q7884: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
