-- Q8206: Tax-Exempt Organizations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), private inurement prohibits organizational earnings from benefiting insiders, while private benefit is broader and covers non-insiders. The founder-director''s excess $320,000 salary is private inurement subject to Section 4958 intermediate sanctions. The friend''s overpriced materials contract is impermissible private benefit threatening exempt status.
Wrong (A): Section 4958 intermediate sanctions apply only to disqualified persons (insiders). The founder''s friend is not an insider, so the materials contract is private benefit, not subject to Section 4958.
Wrong (B): The categories are reversed. The salary to an insider (founder-director) is private inurement, while the contract with a non-insider (friend) is private benefit.
Wrong (D): Organizational discretion does not shield arrangements from scrutiny. Compensation exceeding comparable amounts and above-market contracts both trigger applicable doctrines.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8206;

-- Q8373: Tax-Exempt Organizations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 509(a)(1) and Section 170(b)(1)(A)(i), churches are automatically classified as public charities regardless of their funding sources. The one-third public support test does not apply to churches, schools, or hospitals.
Wrong (A): The one-third support test under Section 509(a)(1) does not apply to churches. Concentrated funding from a single family does not reclassify a church as a private foundation.
Wrong (B): There is no election mechanism for churches to switch from private foundation to public charity status. Churches are public charities by statutory definition, not by election.
Wrong (C): The 27-month advance ruling period relates to new organizations seeking public charity status under the support tests. Churches are exempt from this requirement entirely.$EXPL$
